  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 202 PM EST Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Snow showers across southern Vermont will end this \nafternoon with a brief lull in precipitation for this evening. \nClouds will remain as a reinforcing shortwave will shift south from \nQuebec later tonight into tomorrow. Wrap around moisture associated \nwith the large upper level trough centered across our region will \nprovide enough moisture to sustain upslope snow showers by tomorrow \nmorning. As the shortwave dips into our area by tomorrow morning, \nblocked flow and orographic lift will lead to stratiform snow \nshowers along our northwestern facing slopes in the northern \nAdirondacks and central and northern Greens. As the core of the \nshortwave passes through tomorrow early afternoon, our showery \nactivity will turn more convective as marginal instability develops, \nallowing shower activity to survive the spine of the Greens into the \neastern Greens, and survive the northwest slopes of the Adirondacks \ninto the central northern Adirondacks. Snow showers will be \nstrongest along the spine of the Greens with 3 to 5 inches of more \npowdery snow due to snow ratios around 10-15:1. Into the Adirondacks \n2 to 4 inches is expected, with the highest amounts in the High \nPeaks and northern Franklin County in New York between US-11 and \nSaranac Lake. Snow levels look to initially hover around 1200ft agl, \nbut dynamical cooling could lead to snow level lowering down towards \n500ft, especially by tomorrow night in coordination with diurnal \ncooling. This could push some flakes into the eastern Champlain \nValley where a few tenths of an inch of wet snow are possible. While \nsnow amounts will be localized to the higher terrain, mountain \npasses, and untreated roads may become slick at times. Moreover, \nsome localized power outages may be possible, but due to the drier \ncharacter of the snow, impacts will be limited. \n\nAs our snow turns more convective tomorrow afternoon into Saturday \nnight, Lake Champlain streamers downstream of Lake Champlain look to \ndevelop as cold air aloft will be 10 degrees cooler than the current \nlake temperatures. Precipitation enhancement will be possible in the \ndownstream regions of southern Chittenden and north and central \nAddison Counties. Precipitation types along this corridor will be \nmainly rain closest to the lake, with snow closer to the western \nslopes of the Greens, but dynamical cooling could push precipitation \ntowards more snow closer to the lake at times. Accumulations look to \nbe light as precipitable water will only be around 3 to 4 tenths of \nan inch. The position of any lake streamers are still somewhat \nuncertain, and will be dependent on the prevailing wind at the time, \nbut it looks to be focused in an area between VT-22A and the\nspine of the Greens.\n\nSnow showers will lighten into Sunday, and taper off by Sunday \nevening, departing the highest summits of the Greens and NEK last. \nSolar heating, and gradual clearing will help to melt any snow in \nthe lower elevations by Monday, with a warming and drying trend \nbeginning early next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Confidence remains high that pervasive ridging will \nremain over the region next week keeping local weather dry and \npromoting a warming trend. High temperatures are favored in the 50s \nfrom Tuesday through Friday with lows ranging in the 20s to low 30s \nthrough mid week, climbing to around 30 to upper 30s by the end of \nthe work week. Conditions for radiational cooling will be near \nideal, so typically colder hollows will be running several degrees \ncolder each morning.",
